const topicLibrary = [
 ["Network protocols and latency","Trace DNS, TCP or QUIC, TLS, HTTP, and application work. Serial hops add latency; connection reuse, fewer round trips, and p95 or p99 measurements help locate cost."],
 ["DNS, CDN, and edge design","DNS maps names to endpoints. CDNs cache content near users; edge runtimes can execute small tasks. Plan TTLs, invalidation, origin protection, and regional failover."],
 ["API design and versioning","Define resources, pagination, validation, errors, and idempotency. Prefer compatible additive changes, explicit deprecation, and quotas that protect service capacity."],
 ["Relational data modeling","Use transactions and constraints to protect invariants. Model authoritative records clearly, then index measured queries and use read models when access patterns diverge."],
 ["NoSQL and access patterns","Choose key-value, document, wide-column, or graph stores from the access patterns. Evaluate partition keys, hot keys, consistency, secondary indexes, and operations."],
 ["Database indexes and query plans","Indexes trade storage and write work for faster reads. Inspect query plans, selectivity, lock waits, and tail latency before adding or removing one."],
 ["Replication and consistency","Replication adds read capacity and resilience but introduces lag. Set read-your-writes or eventual consistency by workflow and keep critical invariants on an authority."],
 ["Transactions, isolation, and sagas","Transactions protect local invariants. Across services, use durable sagas with idempotent steps and explicit compensation instead of assuming one global transaction."],
 ["CAP, PACELC, and quorum reasoning","During partitions, systems trade consistency and availability; outside partitions, latency and consistency still trade off. Topology and quorum choices shape real guarantees."],
 ["Consensus and leader election","Consensus coordinates replicas despite faults at a latency cost. Use established systems for coordination and fence stale leaders with epochs or fencing tokens."],
 ["Sharding and partitioning","Choose keys that spread traffic and target common queries. Plan for hot partitions, skew, cross-shard operations, resharding, and tenant movement."],
 ["Caching and invalidation","Select cache-aside, write-through, or refresh patterns by freshness needs. Set TTLs, bound memory, avoid stampedes, and keep the source of truth explicit."],
 ["Queues, streams, and delivery","Queues buffer work; streams retain event histories. Plan for retries, duplicate delivery, ordering scope, consumer lag, dead letters, backpressure, and replay."],
 ["Event-driven design and outbox","Events decouple services but introduce eventual consistency. A transactional outbox prevents lost publishes; consumers still need deduplication and idempotent effects."],
 ["Rate limiting and admission control","Token and leaky buckets shape bursts. Apply limits by identity and cost, return retry hints, and shed low-priority work before critical paths saturate."],
 ["Resilience and graceful degradation","Use deadlines, bounded retries with jitter, circuit breakers, bulkheads, and fallbacks. Degrade optional features while protecting shared capacity."],
 ["Storage sizing, lifecycle, and backup","Estimate growth plus indexes, replicas, logs, and backups. Define retention tiers, test restore time, and meet recovery point and recovery time objectives."],
 ["Multi-region and disaster recovery","Choose active-active or active-passive based on recovery and consistency needs. Define RPO and RTO, replicate required state, test failover, and prevent split brain."],
 ["Security, identity, and privacy","Apply least privilege, encryption, secret rotation, audit trails, data minimization, and deletion policies. Threat model trust boundaries and credential abuse."],
 ["Observability and SLOs","Correlate metrics, logs, and traces. Define user-facing SLIs and SLOs, track error budget, and alert on symptoms that need action."],
 ["Capacity planning and load testing","Estimate average and peak demand, then validate with realistic load. Watch saturation, queue age, tail latency, and failure behavior with headroom."],
 ["Service boundaries and microservices","Split around data ownership and change boundaries. Account for network failure, deployment coordination, observability, and distributed transaction costs."],
 ["Containers, orchestration, and serverless","Choose execution models by startup, scaling, duration, operational ownership, and cost. Health checks, resource limits, rollout safety, and cold starts matter."],
 ["Search and autocomplete","Separate authoritative writes from search indexes. Use asynchronous indexing, ranking, typo tolerance, prefix structures, and explicit freshness targets."],
 ["Real-time systems and WebSockets","Persistent connections reduce polling delay but need routing, heartbeats, reconnect backoff, fanout, presence expiry, and durable catch-up."],
 ["Distributed locks and leases","Leases can expire while a paused owner resumes. Use fencing tokens and bounded leases; prefer database constraints or partition ownership when possible."],
 ["Schema evolution and migrations","Use expand, migrate, contract: add compatible fields, backfill in bounded batches, switch readers and writers, then remove old fields with rollback plans."],
 ["Cost-aware architecture","Compare compute, storage, egress, managed-service, and operational costs against product goals. Include redundancy, bursts, idle capacity, and transfer costs."],
 ["Machine learning inference","Plan model serving, accelerator capacity, batching, feature freshness, rollout, fallback behavior, and latency budgets. Separate online serving from offline training."],
 ["Retrieval and vector search","Combine retrieval, filtering, ranking, and generation within a latency and token budget. Track freshness, access control, evaluation quality, and weak-retrieval fallbacks."],
 ["Data governance and lineage","Track where data comes from, who can use it, and how it changes. Define ownership, classification, lineage, retention, and deletion across derived datasets."],
 ["Concurrency control and optimistic writes","Use versions or compare-and-swap to detect conflicting updates. Define conflict handling and retry limits; keep critical invariants inside atomic operations."],
 ["Backpressure and overload control","Bound queues and in-flight work, expose saturation, and slow or reject producers when consumers fall behind. Unbounded buffering turns overload into delayed failure."],
 ["Blue-green and canary releases","Route a small share of traffic to a new version, compare health and SLOs, and roll back automatically. Make schema and message changes compatible during rollout."],
 ["Tenant isolation and noisy neighbors","Partition resources and quotas by tenant. Measure per-tenant load, isolate expensive work, and design migrations and authorization checks to prevent data leakage."],
 ["Modeling failure domains","Map dependencies to zones, regions, networks, and control planes. Place replicas across independent failure domains and test correlated failures, not only single-node loss."]
];
lessons.push(...topicLibrary.map(([title,body],i)=>({stage:i<6?"05 · Data & storage":i<14?"06 · Distributed systems":i<22?"07 · Reliability & operations":"08 · Advanced topics",level:i<6?"Data":i<14?"Distributed":"Advanced",title,time:"12 min",lede:body.split(". ")[0]+".",sections:[["How to reason about it",body],["Design check","Tie the choice to the workload, user guarantees, failure assumptions, and measurable objectives. Write down the invariant that must hold when a dependency fails."]],takeaway:body,question:"What should guide this architecture decision?",choices:["A familiar technology name","The workload, guarantees, and failure behavior","The largest number of services","An assumption that networks never fail"],answer:1,explanation:"Good choices fit the workload and user guarantees while accounting for failures and operational limits."})));
